items.facts:
- The Standard Model has 3 generations of fermions. Each generation has 4 types: an up-type quark, a down-type quark, a charged lepton and a neutrino. That gives 4 x 3 = 12.
- "12" depends on how you count:
  - with 3 colours per quark: (2x3 + 2) x 3 = 24;
  - adding antiparticles: 48;
  - as chiral (Weyl) fields, using the PDG representations (3,2)+(3̄,1)+(3̄,1)+(1,2)+(1,1): 15 per generation, 45 in total, 48 with right-handed neutrinos.
- Light neutrino species from the invisible Z width:
  - LEP-SLC 2006: N_ν = 2.9840 ± 0.0082, which is 1.95σ below 3;
  - corrected value (Janot and Jadach 2020: improved Bhabha cross-section plus correlated luminosity bias): 2.9963 ± 0.0074, which is 0.50σ from 3. PDG 2026 uses this value;
  - Γ_inv = 499.3 ± 1.5 MeV. This only counts neutrinos that couple to the Z and are lighter than M_Z/2;
  - cosmology's baseline N_eff is 3.044.
- Quarks also come in 3 colours (SU(3)). This connects to the lepton/quark pattern: within each generation the SU(2)^2-U(1)_Y anomaly cancels only if N_c·(1/3) + (-1) = 0, i.e. N_c = 3. Equivalently, the charges in a generation sum to zero: N_c(2/3 - 1/3) - 1 = 0. This works generation by generation, so it says nothing about how many generations there are.
- Why there are 3 generations is unexplained in the Standard Model. Known constraints:
  - CP violation in quark mixing needs at least 3 generations, because there are (n-1)(n-2)/2 CP phases: 0 for n = 2, 1 for n = 3. This is the Kobayashi-Maskawa result, Nobel 2008: "at least three families of quarks."
  - QCD asymptotic freedom needs 33 - 2n_f > 0, so n_f ≤ 16, i.e. at most 8 generations.
  - No known principle fixes the number at exactly 3.
- There is another real "12": the 12 gauge bosons (8+3+1). But they group as 8/3/1, not 4/4/4.

- All evidence for dark matter is gravitational. The PDG 2026 review lists stellar velocity dispersions, rotation curves, the virial theorem, gravitational lensing, the CMB and structure formation. Dark matter is detected because it gravitates, i.e. it curves spacetime and moves within it. So it is inside spacetime by construction; "outside spacetime" has no operational meaning in general relativity.
- Dark matter is 26.4% of the critical density and 84.4% of all matter (PDG). Planck gives Ω_c h^2 = 0.1200 ± 0.0012; the P-ACT-LB2 combination gives 0.1174 ± 0.0006. These two datasets differ slightly.
- Local density near the Sun, where sources disagree:
  - de Salas and Widmark 2021: 0.4–0.6 GeV/cm^3 from local analyses, 0.3–0.5 from global ones;
  - PDG 2026 (Baudis and Profumo, revised Aug 2025): 0.2–0.6 from global methods, 0.3–1.5 from Gaia, 0.47 ± 0.05 from a normalising-flow study, 0.55 ± 0.17 from an updated halo model;
  - Famaey 2015 suggests about 0.5.
- Dark-matter mass inside a 70-L body at any instant:
  - at 0.3 GeV/cm^3: 2.1e4 GeV = 3.7e-23 kg = 2.24e4 proton masses;
  - at 0.4 GeV/cm^3: 2.8e4 GeV = 5.0e-23 kg = 2.98e4 proton masses;
  - across the full 0.2–0.6 range: 2.5e-23 to 7.5e-23 kg;
  - that is about 7e-25 of a 70-kg body.
- How many particles that is depends on the unknown particle mass. At 0.4 GeV/cm^3: 280 particles if 100 GeV, 700 if 40 GeV, 28,000 if 1 GeV.
- It does not stay in the body: it moves at about v_c = 233 km/s (PDG), crossing 1 m in 4.3 µs.
- Any non-gravitational interaction is tightly limited. LZ (4.2 tonne·yr; Phys. Rev. Lett. 135, 011802 (2025)) gives σ_SI < 2.2e-48 cm^2 at 40 GeV.
- A crude upper bound on scatters in the body. Assumptions: the whole 70 kg treated as oxygen-16; coherent A^2 scaling; no form factor or detection threshold; ρ = 0.4 GeV/cm^3; v = 233 km/s. Result: fewer than 1.5e-3 scatters per year, i.e. fewer than one every ~650 years. This is order-of-magnitude only.
- Nothing in physics singles out death. A body's atoms persist and keep interacting electromagnetically before and after death (protons are stable beyond 1e34 yr in the p→e+π0 mode). A dark-matter link to the afterlife has no testable mechanism, so it is outside physics.
